Hi Arron,


Thank you for your email, and for your patience. We have been discussing the matter internally. First and foremost, we are grateful
 to have Zoe on board, and it has been an extremely meaningful collaboration. We also look forward to having Jennifer join the team.


We understand the perceived risk of Counsel having to wait for years for uplifted fees to become payable while other cases remain
 unresolved. To reassure you, that is not how the CFA operates.


Please&nbsp;see a detailed note on the operation of the CFA below. The uplifted rate is triggered by the success of the majority of Clients
 (and their claims) within each GLO. As such, Counsel’s uplift is applied progressively as each GLO succeeds. This structure avoids an “all or nothing” outcome at the very end and ensures that Counsel’s credit risk is minimised at every stage.


We would be grateful if you could consider the note below, which we hope will assuage your concerns. Of course, we remain available
 to discuss any of these&nbsp;points if helpful.


Kind regards,
Nish

&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Explanation of the Operation of the CFA
&nbsp;
1. Categories of Counsel’s Work

Work done by Counsel in the NOx litigation falls into four pools, depending on which Clients benefit:



Pan-NOx Costs&nbsp;– work benefiting all claims across the entire litigation.



Lead &amp; ALGLO Costs&nbsp;– work benefiting all claims in the Lead (Mercedes) and ALGLO (Ford, Nissan-Renault, Peugeot-Citroën) group actions.



Sub-Group Costs&nbsp;– work benefiting a subset of claims across more than one GLO (e.g. French Blocking Statute applying only to Renault/Nissan and
 Peugeot/Citroën).



GLO-Specific Costs&nbsp;– work benefiting Clients in a single GLO only (e.g. BMW).


The current Pan-NOx CFA covers Pan-NOx, Lead/ALGLO costs and Sub-Group Costs. The GLO-specific CFAs are being prepared separately
 so that all categories of Counsel’s work are covered.


In practice, Counsel may perform work falling into different categories on the same day (e.g. drafting an email&nbsp;for Pan-NOx,
 attending a meeting for Lead &amp; ALGLO, and taking a call on BMW GLO-specific issues). Please note that the classification depends on the nature and benefit of the work, not on how it is labelled in advance.

&nbsp;
2. Apportionment of Costs

Counsel’s fees are not billed to individual Clients directly but shared proportionately according to the number of vehicles
 they have in the relevant pool*:



Pan-NOx&nbsp;– apportioned across 1,845,151 claims.



Lead &amp; ALGLO&nbsp;– apportioned across 970,660 claims.



Sub-Group&nbsp;– apportioned across the subset of claims only.



GLO-specific&nbsp;– apportioned within the relevant GLO (e.g. 264,550 claims in BMW).


*Claim figures above taken from the 2 September 2025 Group Register.

&nbsp;
3. Definition of Success (para 19 CFA)

Success&nbsp;is assessed client-by-client, litigation-by-litigation:



A client succeeds if they recover damages (by settlement, judgment, or otherwise) for more than 50% of the vehicles&nbsp;they have registered in that
 GLO.



If a client succeeds, Counsel’s share of fees attributable to that client is payable at the uplifted rate&nbsp;(Base Rate + 30%).



If they do not succeed, Counsel is paid only at the discounted rate&nbsp;(Base Rate – 30%).


Example:



Client A has 10 vehicles in Mercedes. If 6 or more recover damages, Counsel is uplifted on that client’s share. If 5 or fewer recover, only the
 discounted rate applies.

&nbsp;
4. Why Counsel Cannot Claim Uplift Across the Board

The&nbsp;CFA deliberately disaggregates Counsel’s work:



Success in one GLO does not carry across to others.



Counsel cannot argue that all common work is tied to the Lead &amp; ALGLO cases alone.



Instead, Counsel’s fees for Pan-NOx, Lead &amp; ALGLO, and Sub-Group&nbsp;work are allocated across all Clients&nbsp;(para 22), and each Client’s share is
 uplifted or discounted depending on whether that Client’s own claim meets the success test.


This&nbsp;ensures Counsel’s uplift is proportionate and tied to actual outcomes, not just the fortunes of the Lead &amp; ALGLOs.

&nbsp;
5. Practical Effect


Counsel’s remuneration will be a blend&nbsp;of uplifted and discounted fees, depending on which Clients succeed in which GLO.



Success in one GLO (e.g. Mercedes) triggers uplift on the Mercedes Clients’ share of Counsel’s fees immediately&nbsp;upon conclusion of that GLO.
 So that means the Mercedes Clients’ share of Pan-NOx, Lead &amp; ALGLO, Sub-Group and GLO-specific costs.



Counsel does not have to wait until all 13 GLOs conclude to get their uplifted rate for the cases that have been successful.


Other GLOs remain at the discounted rate until they themselves achieve success.
