Hi Anna, Shahrina
&nbsp;
Many thanks for those lists. We have considered and given where we are, with applications on this due by 8 January 2026, we think that it is best if we
 contact all of the below experts as well as those that we had identified initially to gauge (i) fit; and (ii) availability. We would propose that PG contact the two people n their list; Hausfeld contact the four people below; and LD contact the four we identified.
 To streamline matters, we have set out below a draft email – let us know any issues with that, otherwise LD will contact the people on our list this evening given that many are US-based. We should be grateful if the PG and Hausfeld teams could similarly contact
 their respective lists asap.
&nbsp;
To note, we consider that it would be most efficient to send an email enquiry now to gauge (i) general availability; and (ii) general fit (at a very high-level)
 of course. Once we know whether prospective experts (i) think that the task at hand would fit their expertise; and (ii) are able to commit timewise, we agree that we will then want to set up introductory / scoping calls asap. &nbsp;
&nbsp;
Draft email
&nbsp;

Dear X

&nbsp;

We are reaching out to you as we are in need of a consumer behaviour expert.

&nbsp;

We write in connection with the Pan-NOx Emissions Group Litigation (https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cjr5epw8dweo)
 in which we act for the Claimants.

&nbsp;

We have set out below some more information about the case and what we are looking for.

&nbsp;

Brief summary of the case

&nbsp;

The central allegation of the Claimants is that the Lead Defendants optimised NOx emissions of certain diesel vehicles in legally required testing conditions, but that the vehicles did not achieve
 these same reductions in emissions in real-world conditions when the vehicles were driven outside testing conditions.

&nbsp;

The PDD Trial involves five “lead” cases being brought against Mercedes-Benz, Ford, Nissan, Renault and Peugeot/Citroen &nbsp;(together the “Lead Defendants”). It is a key milestone in the wider
 Dieselgate emissions claims in the High Court that have been issued against a number of vehicle manufacturers: Mercedes, Vauxhall/Opel, Nissan/Renault, Volkswagen/Porsche, Peugeot/Citroën, Jaguar/Land Rover, Ford, BMW, FCA/Suzuki, Volvo, Hyundai-Kia, Toyota
 and Mazda. These claims are together referred to as the “NOx Group Litigation”.

&nbsp;

The purpose of the PDD Trial is to determine whether 20 Core Sample Vehicles (“CSVs”) manufactured and sold between 2012 and 2017 by the Lead Defendants contained prohibited defeat devices
 (“PDDs”) under the Regulation (EC) 715/2007 (the “Emissions Regulation”), and if they did, whether the presence of such devices gives rise to private law claims for breach of contract and breach of statutory duty in English law. The Court selected
 the CSVs to reflect a broad range of alleged PDDs in order to make the findings as useful as possible for the NOx Group Litigation. Several key issues will be determined at the PDD Trial, which will involve detailed witness and expert evidence. The Lead Defendants
 deny the presence of any PDDs in the CSVs.

&nbsp;

The Court’s findings in the PDD Trial will bind all parties that form part of the Pan-Nox Litigation. A separate quantum trial is scheduled for October 2026 which, depending on the outcome of the
 PDD Trial, will resolve quantum issues for any breach of contract and/or statutory duty claims.

&nbsp;

It is this quantum trial that we need input from a consumer behaviour expert on.

&nbsp;

Outline of what we are looking for

&nbsp;

We are looking for someone who could give expert evidence in English litigation in the quantum trial scheduled for autumn 2026. The exact scope of the instruction can be explained more fully in
 a call, but broadly:

·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
How the presence of PDDs in the vehicles may have impacted consumer choices; and

·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
How much of a discount consumers would expect had they known about the presence of PDDs; and

·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
What market evidence might be required for the above.

&nbsp;

In terms of commitment, this would likely involve (i) providing assistance to us as we develop our case strategy, including design of market evidence (this would be ongoing, but importantly there
 is a deadline on 8 January by which we need to make an application to the Court to justify why we need a consumer behavioural expert so we would need engagement form you as soon as possible); (ii) giving expert evidence in the Court (please note that the trial
 is currently listed for 8 weeks from October 2026 and we would need you to attend Court for between a day and a week); (iii) writing an expert report for the Court (the current deadline of which is May 2026).
&nbsp;

Next steps

&nbsp;

If you consider that you might fit the bill of what we are looking for and are not conflicted, we would like to set up an introductory call at the end of this week / early next week. Similarly,
 if you are not available, but have any recommendation for someone else who may fit the bill, any recommendations will be gratefully received.

&nbsp;

Let us know if you have any questions. We look forward to hearing from you soon.

