Second and third week of trial


&nbsp;


Week two of the PDD Trial focused on the issue of what are conditions which are “reasonably likely to be encountered in normal vehicle operation and use”
 for the purposes of identifying prohibited defeat devices in the Defendants’ vehicles. The Court heard from experts in the field of what constitutes &quot;normal driving&quot; and climatology. For the Claimants, our experts were Professor Tate (normal driving) and Professor
 Mills (climatology) and for the Defendants, Professor Hausberger (normal driving) and Professor Chapman (climatology).&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Ultimately, while this was important evidence which will underpin the definition of &quot;prohibited defeat device&quot;, as the Judge remarked at the end of the evidence
 - it would not be a central part of her ultimate judgment.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


That said, and as we have advised our insurers; we think that the Claimants’ evidence and cross-examination of the Defendants’ experts has clearly had a positive
 impact on the judge’s views of this element of the case. While we anticipate that the likely approach Lady Justice Cockerill will take is to set a limit on the range of conditions which would reasonably be encountered during normal vehicle operation and use,
 however, we do not expect that she will be inclined to adopt an excessively restrictive set of boundaries (which the Defendants have advocated for) in light of the Claimants’ case on this issue.


&nbsp;


Starting tomorrow, Mercedes kicks us off into the manufacturer specific sections of the trial. Mercedes will run from tomorrow, 29 October 2025, until 6 November.
 Ford then starts on 10 November and runs until 18 November. Preparations for Ford are going well. As previously offered, if any of you wish to attend on the 10,11,12,13, 17 or 18 November, please let me know. You are most welcome to attend and we can arrange
 for this to happen.&nbsp;


