Subject: RE: Hyundai-Kia Statute Bill


&nbsp;
Thanks very much indeed this this, Sarah and Jamie.
&nbsp;
I will work with Tom and Jaspreet to try to finalise the statute bill today.
&nbsp;
In the meantime, I have carried out a quick analysis to assess the suitability of JLG’s proposal based on the figures set out.

&nbsp;
I think that the primary point we need to address with this offer is to ensure that we would not seek inadvertently seek to recover costs over and above what we are actually entitled
 to charge.
&nbsp;
Obviously, we can recover inter partes costs from the Defendants. Under our CFA we can also recover from damages i) the shortfall in recovery of costs and disbursements ii) a success
 fee of 100%. The overall deductions which can be made from damages are capped at 50% under our CFA.
&nbsp;
JLG’s proposed offer would equate to a costs recovery of £91,781,155.54 across PG’s whole cohort. My analysis indicates that, absent the 50% cap, we would be permitted to recover
 in the region of £290,940,303 in respect of inter partes costs and deductions from damages under the CFA. Our clients would of course be protected by 50% cap imposed by our CFA at the £91,781,155.54, that being 50% of the total payment.
&nbsp;
Based on this, it seems that the costs we have actually incurred and can recover (from a combination of inter partes costs and deductions from damages under the CFA) are well in
 excess of the amounts claimed in the JLG offer. As such, it doesn’t seem to me that there is any risk that the numbers won’t add up for PG on this point.
&nbsp;
It is worth noting that for the purposes of this analysis, I have calculated the CFA cap on the assumption that in reality the ‘damages’ per claim is £4,503.16, of which 50% is claimed
 as costs. In the event of a challenge on the basis that damages are in fact the net ‘in hand compensation’ referred to in the offer of £2,251.58, the cap on deductions we can make from Claimants under the CFA would be halved to £45,890,577. In those circumstances,
 it seems that the offers the offer would still be feasible in principle, as the inter partes recoverable costs total £72,842,060.45. The total of inter partes costs (72,842,060.45) and costs deducted from damages (£45,890,577) therefore totals £118,732,637.45,
 which still exceeds the proposed costs allocation of £91,781,155.54 set out in the JLG offer by some margin.

&nbsp;
If anybody disagrees to the extent where the JLG offer may not be viable or appropriate, do let me know.
&nbsp;
Thanks
&nbsp;
