Helpful response from Charlie. I think we should put this response in writing given they wanted their demands in writing before they’d agree to any meeting…&nbsp;&nbsp;&#8203;&#8203;&#8203;&#8203;Alicia&nbsp;AliniaChief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629From: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 08 October 2025 09:20To: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Cc: Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: Re: LD Agency&nbsp;Clyde's advice on their latest set of demands:&nbsp;&nbsp;________________The legal advice that you have received is privileged - you are not going to waive privilege;I would be minded to say the following to them in a meeting tomorrow:Clause 4.2(c) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “comply with all reasonable and lawful directions given to the Employee by the Company” – they have been given reasonable instructions as to how to proceed and they now need to follow them;Further, Clause 4.2(b) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “diligently exercise such powers and perform such duties as may from time to time be assigned to the Employee by the Company;” – this is the same point.To the extent that you need you could also specify that their contracts dictate that they shall serve the Company as&nbsp;a&nbsp;Senior&nbsp;Associate&nbsp;and will report&nbsp;to&nbsp;Legal Director or their nominee.” You can tell inform them that Leigh Day is the designated “nominee” for the purposes of their contracts.Their contracts also stipulate that they must “use&nbsp;their&nbsp;best-endeavours&nbsp;to&nbsp;promote,&nbsp;protect,&nbsp;develop&nbsp;and extend&nbsp;the business of the Company even during their notice periods.” Their actions are not in keeping with this.Finally, and I don’t know whether you want to go there, but all this distraction at a critical time cannot be in the best interests of the clients and the case. PG has listened to all of their concerns and addressed them. They now need to crack on with the case for the benefit of the clients.&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718From:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Tuesday, October 07, 2025 6:33 PMTo:&nbsp;Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;FW: Re: LD Agency &nbsp;We’re not doing this anymore. We’re not providing them with our employment advice nor are we sharing anything further with Ben.&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Alicia&nbsp;AliniaChief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629From:&nbsp;Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;07 October 2025 18:15To:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: Re: LD Agency&nbsp;Dear Anna&nbsp;Thank you for your email. We would be grateful if you would share with us the employment advice received from counsel, as referred to below, along with the advice received from Ben and Theo (including their responses to the points raised by the case leads in respect of the agreement previously).&nbsp;&nbsp;Please also let us know if it is Ben and Theo's view that the statements made in Court yesterday should be corrected, in light of the fact that the case leads had not approved the agreement of which he spoke and for which it was implied we had.&nbsp;&nbsp;Please do share this with us before the proposed call so that we can consider properly the revised agreement and your email below.&nbsp;&nbsp;Thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Georgina&nbsp;FallowsLegal&nbsp;DirectorFrom:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Tuesday, October 7, 2025 5:39 PMTo:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: Re: LD Agency&nbsp;Dear All,&nbsp;Thank you for all the points you have raised on the proposed Agency Agreement. To confirm, I have shared your concerns with Ben W KC and Theo at your request.&nbsp;We attach the final version agreed with Leigh Day, which has been modified in material ways since the last version you saw.&nbsp; It takes into account some of the key comments you have shared, incorporating some changes as a result. We intend to move forward to execute later today. &nbsp;&nbsp;Definition of Common Work. Per paragraph 5, PG will supervise its employees (including you during your notice period) in respect of all&nbsp;work, whether delegated by LD or otherwise. This ensures continuity and means that you can continue to work on non-Common Work (e.g. PG only work) and anything else.Pan NOx Agency Agreement. References to the Pan NOx Agency Agreement have been removed and the one definition used from it is now set out without reference to the Pan NOx AA. This had been the intention from inception but Martyn shared a working draft.Step-Down Date. This has been amended to indicate that the court order will approve PG’s application, not the resignation itself.Clarification re. supervision. As to your concerns about “dual supervision” by LD and PG and compatibility with ongoing professional and regulatory duties to PG clients, this has been resolved now by the latest amendments. These make clear that: (a) as long as you work for PG, you remain subject to PG’s supervision at all times regarding any work you do (delegated Common Work or otherwise); and (b) PG authorises you to do Common Work under the direction of and reporting to LD as LS.&nbsp; We have removed the word “solely” in both cases, to make clear that PG continues to supervise and be responsible for you.&nbsp;PG have taken specialist employment law advice. The agreement, and what you are being asked to do, is consistent with your employment agreements and your rights as employees.&nbsp;We have not accepted your proposal that PG personnel can also take direction from a third party authorised by LD. We can discuss this further, but PG needs to maintain knowledge and meaningful involvement/control over who can give directions to its personnel – and this is right now limited to LD in its capacity as Lead Solicitor, and a firm with which we have worked significantly on this matter. LD is content with this, and it should not raise any concerns regarding your workstreams. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As concerns asking junior members of staff to send out correspondence, there is nothing inappropriate about this - and while ideally correspondence would be sent out during business hours, that is not out of the ordinary on NOX.&nbsp;&nbsp;As already noted, I will share the final version. I hope you are clear and satisfied that we have taken all your comments into account as appropriate in order to reach a viable framework for PG’s ongoing role. Agreeing the Agency Agreement is clearly now a priority in order to ensure clarity on next steps for the PDD trial and the proper framework in the best interests of our clients. To be clear, while we welcome and value your input, we do not consider that we need your consent to enter into the agreement with Leigh Day.&nbsp;We have all discussed this a good deal in writing. I suggest we have a call about this tomorrow and before speaking with Leigh Day. Could you let me know when might work?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;It remains for me to thank you again for all your enormous efforts to get this case to where it is today. We look forward to continuing to work together under this framework for the duration of the PDD trial.&nbsp;BestAnna&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Anna&nbsp;VargaPartner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;699From:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Sunday, October 5, 2025 4:38 PMTo:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: LD Agency&nbsp;Dear Anna,&nbsp;Thank you for your email.&nbsp;We have some overarching points to raise before addressing the points in your email regarding the Side Letter Agency Agreement (“SL AA”).&nbsp;First and foremost, we have raised serious and significant issues regarding how traumatic we, and the entire team, are finding this process and that we have genuine concerns for the mental health and well-being of our staff. We further flagged that the proposal creates a two-tier structure amongst the wider team, which risks alienating individual members of the staff. Your email worryingly does not address any of those concerns, nor does it even mention them.&nbsp;We also note that various junior staff within the NOx department have been enlisted to send out correspondence, including on weekends, relating to non-common work and non-PDD Trial matters (see, for example, an email from Hannah Varghese at 15.32 on Saturday, 4 October 2025). Requiring the most junior staff to become actively involved in non-common work issues, while on the other hand attempting to assert that “everything we have done or are intending to do is with the best interests of our clients as well as our staff in mind first and foremost” is simply unreal. Correspondence of that nature and related to non-common work should be sent by an individual not working on critical workstreams.&nbsp;&nbsp;Each of the case leads are committed to finding a co-operative solution to the issues that are being raised. That is why we are proactively engaging in discussions and, in aid of assisting the firm, why we have continued to raise points so as to avoid the firm falling foul of issues that they may otherwise be presented with, e.g. noting potential additional disclosure obligations re. documents referred to in witness statements. You also have said that you “welcome any specific input we wish to contribute”. This is difficult to contemplate as being a genuine offer in circumstances where a) the SL AA was provided by Leigh Day (“LD”), not you nor the Board, despite Pogust Goodhead (“PG”) being our employer; and b) where we have previously sought to provide input (e.g. meeting with Alicia Alinia at 18:00 Monday 29 September 2025) and you have not engaged with it. Instead, you have sent us an email that dismisses our concerns and informs us that our description of the SL AA is incorrect, while at the same time stating that your intention is for this email to now bring these discussions to a conclusion. This is neither helpful nor is it in the spirit of co-operation.&nbsp;&nbsp;For the avoidance of any doubt, we do not consent to the SL AA in its current form. No representations can be made to the Court at the PTR on 6 October that this has been put in place and is agreed. To the extent that you and the Board believe you can bind individuals named in the SL AA (or annexes) by virtue of it being a ‘firm decision’ we disagree for reasons set out below. Every individual subject to the agreement either does not consent to it (the case leads) or, as far as we are aware, has never seen any version of it and so is unable to have provided consent.&nbsp; We accept of course, that Counsel can inform the Court (on 6 October or otherwise) that it is being drafted and the intention is that it will be put in place in due course.&nbsp;&nbsp;If we, as the collective case leads, are to work collaboratively with the Board and you, and HR, to establish how we can move forward amicably, then it requires serious engagement from you and the Board, and not a dismissal of our concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;We trust that this email will allow for a more collaborative approach going forward.&nbsp;On the points in your email:&nbsp;1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Purpose and Effect of the Side Letter&nbsp;You explained that PG’s proposed role is intended to operate “exactly as envisaged” within the existing Pan-NOx AA framework, and that the SL AA will simply implement a delegation of common work from LD to PG personnel. If that were indeed the case, a separate SL AA would not be necessary; indeed, this accords with our own understanding that the new arrangement is entirely novel.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;You say this agreement is to “ensure that [we] can continue to work as currently on the existing work plans.” Having reviewed the SL AA, it is clear that it goes further than the delegation provisions in the Pan-NOx AA, and that is precisely why it requires a standalone agreement. The SL AA imposes materially different terms in at least two key respects:&nbsp;&nbsp;a. Formal separation of supervision and control&nbsp;The SL AA introduces an unusual structure whereby PG personnel will perform common work solely under the direction of LD, but remain employed and supervised by PG. Indeed, we understand that the Board intends for PG personnel to carry out generic/firm-specific work under PG’s direction. This formalises a split between line management and professional control that is not addressed, let alone authorised, in the Pan-NOx AA. That agreement permits delegation of work to another firm, not delegation of the supervision of one firm’s employees to another while the employer retains all employment obligations.&nbsp;&nbsp;b. Governance over PG’s internal personnel decisions&nbsp;The SL AA (via Annex B) requires LD’s consultation and agreement before PG can add or remove PG personnel from common work. That means PG cannot unilaterally deploy or reassign its own staff in this litigation without LD’s approval. This imposes external governance over PG’s workforce, a control that is not contemplated in the Pan-NOx AA and which cuts across PG’s autonomy as an employer.&nbsp;&nbsp;The SL AA changes the status quo in material ways such that it cannot be said that what is being proposed is “exactly what we have all been working under” or that “this will not practically change much day to day.”&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tension with Employment Contracts&nbsp;Because the SL AA goes beyond what is authorised in the Pan-NOx AA, it creates direct tension with the terms of PG employment contracts. You advise that “there will be no change to your employment contract” and that “consent by employees is not necessary for PG to execute the agency agreement.” We respectfully disagree.&nbsp;Clause 2(b) of the SL AA states that “PG Personnel shall perform such Common Work … solely under the direction of, and reporting solely to, Leigh Day.” This directly conflicts with multiple provisions that we presume appear in each individual employee contract:&nbsp;&nbsp;a. Duty of obedience and supervision&nbsp;Under Clause 4/Duties of the employment contract (which we presume is a standard term of contract for all PG employees), PG employees are contractually required to comply with PG’s directions and report to PG line managers. They are to “devote the whole of their time, attention and abilities to the business of the Company.” The SL AA seeks to override that by imposing a structure in which we report solely to LD in respect of a major portion of our work, without any formal secondment or variation of contract.&nbsp;&nbsp;b. Regulatory oversight&nbsp;The split between day-to-day supervision (by LD) and formal employment responsibility (by PG) creates regulatory ambiguity under the SRA Code of Conduct. Who is the supervising solicitor? Are there two supervising solicitors, one from Leigh Day and another from PG? Which firm bears responsibility for ensuring compliance with professional standards?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;These are not academic questions - they carry regulatory and indemnity implications.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;c. Consent and legal clarity&nbsp;The arrangement introduces a dual-control model, but without staff consent, proper transparency, or any framework setting out lines of accountability. As PG remains the employer, yet LD directs our work in practice, the legal and ethical basis for this arrangement is unclear, particularly when conflicts, confidentiality obligations, or errors arise.&nbsp;&nbsp;3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Professional Indemnity Insurance Risk&nbsp;The SL AA structure also raises serious questions around insurance cover. If PG personnel are performing common work solely under LD’s direction (as the SL AA confirms), then it is not clear whose professional indemnity (“PI”) insurance would respond in the event of a professional error. This is in circumstances where PG remains the/our employer and is responsible for holding PI cover for its staff. But LD&nbsp;is envisaged as exercising the day to-day control and making the litigation decisions. The SRA expects supervision and insurance liability to be aligned. That is not the case here.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is not hypothetical. A clear example of this risk occurred this week when LD made a costs offer to the Mercedes Defendants without PG’s knowledge or approval from PG's insurer - despite the fact that PG personnel had prepared the underlying work. If that offer proves problematic, it is unclear whether PG or LD (or both) would be liable and, more importantly, whose insurer would be on risk. That ambiguity creates risk for both firms and undermines the clarity of the delegation framework set out in the Pan-NOx AA.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is precisely why the Pan-NOx AA structured delegation at the firm level: to avoid splitting supervision and risk in a way that confuses accountability. The SL AA unpicks that structure and introduces unnecessary exposure.&nbsp;&nbsp;We refer you to 17(a) of the PTR skeleton argument filed by Mercedes where the Defendants have drawn the Court’s attention to this issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Client Consent&nbsp;If PG wishes for us and other PG staff to be instructed by, and report solely to, a firm other than PG, then this requires our express consent, and (we believe) the informed consent of the clients whom we act on behalf of (which will not only be just the clients of PG, but&nbsp; may extent to all clients within every GLO if the work is to be Pan-NOx). If PG personnel are to carry out work on those clients’ cases under the direction of a different firm, the clients must be told who is supervising that work and must provide informed instructions to that effect. We refer you to Rule 1.4 of the Code of Conduct, which requires solicitors to ensure clients are in a position to make informed decisions about their representation. You cannot unilaterally impose this on us, or on any of the PG staff, nor PG’s clients as your email&nbsp; suggests you can.&nbsp;&nbsp;You cannot unilaterally impose a change of supervisory firm on your employees or on the clients, as your email equally suggests. The assumption that this arrangement can simply be implemented without individual or client-level engagement is not supported by the regulatory framework. This concern is not novel: post our comments in this regard, this is also expressly identified in the skeleton argument filed by Mercedes at paragraph 17(b), where they raise the potential for regulatory impropriety in precisely these circumstances.&nbsp;Turning to other points specifically raised in your email:&nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; You note that we are obliged to work our notice periods. Thank you for pointing this out again, although we note it is not consistent with representations that PG has made to the Defendants and Court that the timing of our last days are subject to ongoing discussion. You go on to say that once our notice period has expired, we are free to commence working for JLG, should we so choose. Collectively, none of us have informed you of our plans post-PG.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; You have advised that LD and counsel have approved this solution. Please provide us with this correspondence (including the advice the firm has taken from counsel – as originally requested). We note that as per Martyn Day’s email on 3 October at 13.56 that “Alicia has asked that we leave these issues for PG to sort out with the team. We await the outcome of those discussions and so no need for the meeting this afternoon.” In circumstances where LD are waiting for PG to “sort out these issues with the team” and that they will “await the outcome”, we fail to understand how LD have approved the solution.&nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; You advise that you are “now interviewing so with time this work-stream will be handed over”. Please explain which work-stream? Does this relate to non-common work?&nbsp;We repeat that we are keen to try and work together to find a collaborative solution to these issues, not least because of the importance of the upcoming PDD Trial beginning in a little over a week’s time and for which we are all deeply invested in and, in truth, where all of this is causing a huge and detrimental distraction. However, it is plain to us that the SL AA is plainly more than a simple delegation mechanism. It alters the control and supervision structure, restricts PG’s governance over its own staff, creates clear tension with employment contracts and regulatory obligations, and places work done on behalf of the PG&nbsp;clients under the instruction of an external law firm (without any proposal for client consent to be obtained). If there is to be a material change, it needs to be handled transparently and within a proper legal framework. We raise these points constructively and in good faith, with the shared goal of ensuring compliance and continuing to act in our clients’ best interests.&nbsp;We trust these concerns now demonstrate that the issues raised in the draft SL AA document need to be engaged with, and we look forward to hearing from you on those issues and how they can be addressed, such that we can move forward, together.&nbsp;&nbsp; The Case Leads&nbsp;Michael Burke, Priyanka Gladson, Matthew Hunt, Sarah Bingham &amp; Georgina Fallows&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Sarah&nbsp;BinghamLegal&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7484&nbsp;085&nbsp;187From:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Saturday, October 04, 2025 14:12To:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: LD Agency&nbsp;All,I&nbsp;have been discussing with Alicia and it may be worth clarifying some matters.&nbsp;First, it is important to clarify precisely how the Agency Agreement will work – which is not as you describe.&nbsp; We hope this gives you immediate comfort with the approach.PG’s proposed role is exactly as envisaged in the Pan NOx Agency Agreement, which we have all been working under: PG will, as authorised by the Pan NOx Agency Agreement, act by authorisation of LD with respect to the Common Work delegated to it by LD.&nbsp; The Agency Agreement will enable PG to continue working on the liability trial and quantum workstream while you remain with us working your notice period - this agreement&nbsp;is intended to ensure that you can continue to work as currently on the existing work plans with the Lead Solicitor. &nbsp;With Alicia, I will continue to have strategic input and meaningful involvement.&nbsp;It is a pragmatic solution to the various concerns.Whilst LD will retain the title of sole Lead Solicitor, this will not practically change much day to day.&nbsp; LD will be required to consult with us in connection with decisions and we expect matters to continue in the manner they have to date -&nbsp;your working relationships with LD will be unchanged in that regard. Structurally and to secure PG’s continued involvement in the cases, PG employees will be delegated work by Leigh Day. You know better than most that PG and LD have been working together for years, including on behalf of many other solicitor firms, and&nbsp;this Agency Agreement will mean that PG to will continue doing so whilst not retaining the title of Lead Solicitor.&nbsp;For the avoidance of doubt, there will be no change to your employment contract and Leigh Day do not and will have no employer-employee relationship with you.&nbsp; In the circumstances consent by employees is not necessary for PG to execute the agency agreement, as this is a structural rather than substantive change it should not be an issue.As continuing PG employees you will continue to work on the Non-Common Work streams as required and directed solely by PG (which includes matters related to the non-Lead Cases, which are not subject to the Agency Agreement).&nbsp;If you have any regulatory concerns that you wish to inform the clients of, please do let us know specifically what they are. In addition we are now interviewing so with time this work-stream will be gradually handed over - in a sensible way that serves our clients' best interests.&nbsp;We, LD and counsel have approved this solution which adheres with and is entirely consistent with the existing legal framework, offering the best path forward in the best interests of our clients. We also welcome any specific input you may wish to contribute.&nbsp;As to your future employment, as we have previously explained, you are obliged to work until end of your notice period, in accordance with the terms of your contract.&nbsp; Once your notice period has expired, you will be free to commence working for Johnson Law Group, together with Peter Gallagher, should you so choose.&nbsp; In this specific and limited regard, your covenants will not be enforced.Finally, the firm is very mindful of its professional and regulatory responsibilities and would never expect you to do anything to compromise those. The firm will always supervise its lawyers and safeguard them from potential breaches, and we trust you will do so at all times too, both on your own behalf and on behalf of those you supervise. We do not intend doing anything, nor have we done anything, that would put us or you in breach of any regulatory or professional requirements which remain an absolute priority to us. Everything we have done or are intending to do is with the best interests of our clients as well as our staff in mind first and foremost.We very much hope that this email can now draw these discussions to a conclusion so that we all focus on the imminent trial and delivering the best results to our clients.Best, Anna&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Anna&nbsp;VargaPartner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;699From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Friday, October 3, 2025 12:28 PMTo:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;LD Agency&nbsp;Dear Board, Anna,&nbsp;We have been sent a copy of the draft Agency Agreement by LD late last night and we are considering this. As an initial point, the definition of &quot;Common Work&quot; is not wide enough to capture all common work. It is currently a vague halfway house that doesn't offer clarity or certainty, and we suspect will further encourage the Defendants (and other interested parties) to complain and/or ask more questions.&nbsp;In addition, we cannot stress enough how traumatic we and the entire team are finding this process, and that it is happening on the eve of trial. That is exacerbated by the fact that we are only properly notified of proposed fundamental changes to our employment through Martyn Day and his team, who as a third party, is not our employer, and where it appears to us these changes are being pushed through as a fait accompli publicly, yet without the case leads consultation and agreement.&nbsp;It is massively unfair on each of us, it is not creating a good working environment and may prejudice our ability to produce the level of work required for this trial.&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Before we are in a position to give our agreement (which for the avoidance of doubt we consider necessary, as with all named personnel, before the agreement is capable of execution), we have a number of points that require clarification:&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; we be provided sight of the advice from Ben Williams KC (and any other applicable counsel) who have advised on the AA;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; before the agreement is consented to (at least by the case leads) that there is an agreed and defined role for Pete Gallagher in the individual and pan-NOx litigations, documented (and signed) alongside the AA;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; how the claimant committees, and wider claimant cohort, will be informed that the teams with conduct of their cases are under dual supervision of Martyn Day, and Alicia Alina, in respect of the different spheres of work (common / generic); and·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A &quot;step back&quot; clause is included such as to allow the case leads or team to step back from the AA if the personnel consider their regulatory / professional obligations are untenable with instructions given to them.&nbsp;We also note that the intended agreement covers a list of PG personnel wider than just the case leads (the list provided by LD to Michael Burke at 8.23PM last night has 18 people on it). If it is thought that the list should be wider than just the case leads, which we have expressly disagreed with, we are concerned that by not including the entire department, it creates a two-tier team in PG which will not be conducive for anyone. To seek to split the team will only exacerbate this as it risks alienating individuals. We have genuine concerns for the mental health and well-being of our staff in any event with the ongoing upheaval and the constant attacks by the press and Defendants, and to create an artificial split among the team risks compounding the issues that they may be having. The list of PG personnel should either be only the case leads of Michael Burke, Priyanka Gladson, Georgie Fallows, Matthew Hunt and Sarah Bingham (our preference), or the entire team.&nbsp;Additional to this, we trust that each individual will have their individual consent sought before the agreement can be put in place. If the agreement is sought to be incepted prior to consent being sought, this could constitute a breach of contract for all those it is purported to apply to.&nbsp;Thanks,&nbsp;Case Leads&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;"}

